  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1205 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nAn upper trough is currently sliding across the Rockies into the \nPlains with an embedded shortwave traversing across the Desert \nSouthwest towards the Southern Rockies. At the surface, a low is \npositioned over Ontario with a cold front draping down through the \nMississippi Valley and into western Kansas, connecting to another \nlow over the Texas Panhandle. This cold front will progress south \nlate this afternoon into tonight as the upper shortwave moves \ntowards the region. This will bring widespread rain showers to the \narea with some thunderstorms as well. Model guidance generally has \nMUCAPE values between 300-700 J/kg with the highest values in south-\ncentral KS. Mid-level lapse rates will steepen to greater than 7 \nC/km with 40 kts of effective shear. The overall severe weather \nthreat is very low, though an isolated strong storm cannot be ruled \nout in far southern Kansas for late this afternoon and evening but \nagain chances are extremely low. If a strong storm were to develop, \nthe main hazards would be gusty winds and small hail. As we move \ninto the overnight hours and early Sunday morning, rain chances will \ngradually decrease from west to east as the the upper shortwave and \nassociated surface low continue to shift east. \n\nBy mid Sunday morning, drier conditions are expected to settle into \nour area. Breezy northerly winds will follow the front and continue \nthrough Sunday afternoon along with cooler conditions. Highs for \nSunday are forecast in the 40s to near 50. Lows for Sunday night \nwill drop to near normal for this time of year with readings mainly \nin the 20s. \n\nAs we continue into early next week, another trough will \nslide across the Plains on Tuesday, though any precipitation \nassociated with this trough will stay well west of our area in far \nwestern Kansas/eastern Colorado, as the column remains dry over \neastern Kansas. Below normal temperatures for this time of year are \nprogged for Tuesday and Tuesday night with highs in the lower 40s \nand lows in the lower 20s. \n\nFor Wednesday and beyond, a warming trend will commence as upper \nridging builds in with thickness responding accordingly and \nincreasing. By Friday, highs will return to the 50s with upper 50s \nforecast for next Saturday. Similarly, low temperatures are forecast \nto rise back into the 30s. Mostly dry conditions are expected \nthrough this period as well.",
